### What is TransactionSynchronization?

`TransactionSynchronization` is an interface that Spring provides for registering callbacks tied to the transaction lifecycle. You implement the interface and register it with `TransactionSynchronizationManager.registerSynchronization()` inside an active transaction. The callbacks fire at specific points: before commit, after commit, after rollback, and after completion.

```java
package com.bank.service;

import org.springframework.stereotype.Service;
import org.springframework.transaction.annotation.Transactional;
import org.springframework.transaction.support.TransactionSynchronization;
import org.springframework.transaction.support.TransactionSynchronizationManager;
import java.math.BigDecimal;

@Service
public class TransferService {

    private final AccountRepository accountRepo;
    private final NotificationService notificationService;

    public TransferService(
            AccountRepository accountRepo,
            NotificationService notificationService) {
        this.accountRepo = accountRepo;
        this.notificationService = notificationService;
    }

    @Transactional(rollbackFor = Exception.class)
    public void transfer(String fromId, String toId, BigDecimal amount) {
        Account sender = accountRepo.findByIdForUpdate(fromId);
        Account receiver = accountRepo.findByIdForUpdate(toId);
        sender.debit(amount);
        receiver.credit(amount);
        accountRepo.save(sender);
        accountRepo.save(receiver);

        // Register callback: send SMS only after commit
        TransactionSynchronizationManager.registerSynchronization(
            new TransactionSynchronization() {
                @Override
                public void afterCommit() {
                    notificationService.sendSms(toId,
                        "Rs." + amount + " credited to your account");
                }
            }
        );
    }
}
```

Priya receives the SMS only after the transfer is committed. If the transaction rolls back, the callback never fires.

### What are the callback methods?

The `TransactionSynchronization` interface defines several callback methods:

| Method | When it fires | Use case |
|--------|---------------|----------|
| beforeCommit(boolean readOnly) | Before the TX commits | Final validation, flush side effects |
| beforeCompletion() | Before commit or rollback | Cleanup before outcome |
| afterCommit() | After successful commit | Send notifications, publish events |
| afterCompletion(int status) | After commit or rollback | Cleanup regardless of outcome |

The `afterCompletion` status parameter is `STATUS_COMMITTED` (0) or `STATUS_ROLLED_BACK` (1).

```java
package com.bank.service;

import org.springframework.transaction.support.TransactionSynchronization;

public class TransferNotificationSync implements TransactionSynchronization {

    private final String accountId;
    private final String message;
    private final NotificationService notificationService;

    public TransferNotificationSync(
            String accountId, String message,
            NotificationService notificationService) {
        this.accountId = accountId;
        this.message = message;
        this.notificationService = notificationService;
    }

    @Override
    public void afterCommit() {
        notificationService.sendSms(accountId, message);
    }

    @Override
    public void afterCompletion(int status) {
        if (status == STATUS_ROLLED_BACK) {
            System.out.println("Transfer rolled back for " + accountId);
        }
    }
}
```

### How does afterCommit compare to @TransactionalEventListener?

The `afterCommit` callback is the hand-rolled equivalent of `@TransactionalEventListener(phase = TransactionPhase.AFTER_COMMIT)`. Both achieve the same goal: execute code only after the transaction commits.

| Aspect | TransactionSynchronization | @TransactionalEventListener |
|--------|---------------------------|----------------------------|
| Registration | Programmatic (registerSynchronization) | Declarative (annotation) |
| Coupling | Inline in the service | Separate listener class |
| Event publishing | Not needed | Requires ApplicationEventPublisher |
| Flexibility | Full control over all lifecycle hooks | Only commit/rollback/completion |
| Readability | More verbose | Cleaner for simple cases |

```java
package com.bank.event;

import org.springframework.context.event.EventListener;
import org.springframework.stereotype.Component;
import org.springframework.transaction.event.TransactionPhase;
import org.springframework.transaction.event.TransactionalEventListener;

@Component
public class TransferEventListener {

    private final NotificationService notificationService;

    public TransferEventListener(NotificationService notificationService) {
        this.notificationService = notificationService;
    }

    // Equivalent to TransactionSynchronization.afterCommit()
    @TransactionalEventListener(phase = TransactionPhase.AFTER_COMMIT)
    public void onTransferCommitted(TransferCompletedEvent event) {
        notificationService.sendSms(
            event.getToAccountId(),
            "Rs." + event.getAmount() + " credited to your account");
    }
}
```

For simple after-commit notifications, prefer `@TransactionalEventListener` for cleaner separation. For complex lifecycle hooks (beforeCommit + afterRollback + afterCompletion), use `TransactionSynchronization`.

### How do you use TransactionSynchronization with TransactionTemplate?

You can register synchronization callbacks inside a `TransactionTemplate` callback. This combines programmatic transaction control with lifecycle hooks.

```java
package com.bank.service;

import org.springframework.stereotype.Service;
import org.springframework.transaction.PlatformTransactionManager;
import org.springframework.transaction.support.TransactionSynchronization;
import org.springframework.transaction.support.TransactionSynchronizationManager;
import org.springframework.transaction.support.TransactionTemplate;
import java.math.BigDecimal;

@Service
public class ProgrammaticTransferService {

    private final TransactionTemplate txTemplate;
    private final AccountRepository accountRepo;
    private final NotificationService notificationService;

    public ProgrammaticTransferService(
            PlatformTransactionManager txManager,
            AccountRepository accountRepo,
            NotificationService notificationService) {
        this.txTemplate = new TransactionTemplate(txManager);
        this.accountRepo = accountRepo;
        this.notificationService = notificationService;
    }

    public void transfer(String fromId, String toId, BigDecimal amount) {
        txTemplate.executeWithoutResult(status -> {
            Account sender = accountRepo.findByIdForUpdate(fromId);
            Account receiver = accountRepo.findByIdForUpdate(toId);
            sender.debit(amount);
            receiver.credit(amount);
            accountRepo.save(sender);
            accountRepo.save(receiver);

            TransactionSynchronizationManager.registerSynchronization(
                new TransactionSynchronization() {
                    @Override
                    public void afterCommit() {
                        notificationService.sendSms(toId,
                            "Rs." + amount + " credited");
                    }
                }
            );
        });
    }
}
```

### What happens if afterCommit throws an exception?

An exception thrown inside `afterCommit()` does not roll back the already-committed transaction. The commit is permanent. However, the exception propagates to the caller, which can cause issues if it is unhandled. Always wrap afterCommit logic in a try-catch to prevent post-commit exceptions from disrupting the application.

```java
// WRONG: exception in afterCommit disrupts the caller
TransactionSynchronizationManager.registerSynchronization(
    new TransactionSynchronization() {
        @Override
        public void afterCommit() {
            notificationService.sendSms(toId, message);
            // If SMS service is down, exception propagates to caller
        }
    }
);

// CORRECT: wrap in try-catch
TransactionSynchronizationManager.registerSynchronization(
    new TransactionSynchronization() {
        @Override
        public void afterCommit() {
            try {
                notificationService.sendSms(toId, message);
            } catch (Exception e) {
                log.error("Post-commit notification failed", e);
                // Transaction is already committed, log and move on
            }
        }
    }
);
```

### What are common mistakes?

#### Mistake 1: registering synchronization outside a transaction

```java
// WRONG: no active transaction, registration fails silently or throws
public void notifyWithoutTransaction(String accountId) {
    TransactionSynchronizationManager.registerSynchronization(
        new TransactionSynchronization() {
            @Override
            public void afterCommit() {
                notificationService.sendSms(accountId, "Done");
            }
        }
    );
    // IllegalStateException: Transaction synchronization is not active
}
```

```java
// CORRECT: register inside a @Transactional method or TransactionTemplate callback
@Transactional
public void notifyAfterTransfer(String accountId) {
    TransactionSynchronizationManager.registerSynchronization(
        new TransactionSynchronization() {
            @Override
            public void afterCommit() {
                notificationService.sendSms(accountId, "Done");
            }
        }
    );
}
```

#### Mistake 2: sending notification before commit

```java
// WRONG: notification sent before commit; if TX rolls back, Priya gets a false SMS
@Transactional
public void transfer(String fromId, String toId, BigDecimal amount) {
    Account sender = accountRepo.findByIdForUpdate(fromId);
    Account receiver = accountRepo.findByIdForUpdate(toId);
    sender.debit(amount);
    receiver.credit(amount);
    notificationService.sendSms(toId, "Money received"); // BEFORE commit
    // If commit fails, Priya already got the SMS
}
```

```java
// CORRECT: use afterCommit to send notification only after data is durable
@Transactional
public void transfer(String fromId, String toId, BigDecimal amount) {
    Account sender = accountRepo.findByIdForUpdate(fromId);
    Account receiver = accountRepo.findByIdForUpdate(toId);
    sender.debit(amount);
    receiver.credit(amount);
    TransactionSynchronizationManager.registerSynchronization(
        new TransactionSynchronization() {
            @Override
            public void afterCommit() {
                notificationService.sendSms(toId, "Money received");
            }
        }
    );
}
```

### What are common interview follow-up questions?

- What is the difference between `TransactionSynchronization.afterCommit()` and `@TransactionalEventListener(phase = AFTER_COMMIT)`?
- Can you register multiple synchronization callbacks in the same transaction?
- What happens if `afterCommit()` throws an exception? Does it roll back the transaction?
- How do you test code that depends on `afterCommit()` callbacks?
- When would you choose `TransactionTemplate` over `@Transactional`?
- Can you use `TransactionSynchronization` with a reactive stack (WebFlux)?

### What should a senior engineer know?

- `afterCommit()` runs in the same thread as the transaction, after commit but before the connection is released. Keep it fast; offload heavy work to an async executor.
- In Spring 4.2+, prefer `@TransactionalEventListener` for decoupled after-commit handling. Use `TransactionSynchronization` when you need multiple lifecycle hooks (beforeCommit + afterCompletion) or when you are inside a `TransactionTemplate` callback.
- `TransactionSynchronizationManager.registerSynchronization()` is thread-safe. The registered callbacks are ThreadLocal-scoped and cleared after the transaction completes.
- For batch jobs, register the synchronization inside each `TransactionTemplate.execute()` call so each chunk gets its own notification callback.
- Combining `TransactionSynchronization` with Spring's `ApplicationEventPublisher` gives you both lifecycle awareness and event-driven architecture.

```java
package com.bank.service;

import org.springframework.context.ApplicationEventPublisher;
import org.springframework.stereotype.Service;
import org.springframework.transaction.annotation.Transactional;
import org.springframework.transaction.support.TransactionSynchronization;
import org.springframework.transaction.support.TransactionSynchronizationManager;
import java.math.BigDecimal;

@Service
public class EventDrivenTransferService {

    private final AccountRepository accountRepo;
    private final ApplicationEventPublisher eventPublisher;

    public EventDrivenTransferService(
            AccountRepository accountRepo,
            ApplicationEventPublisher eventPublisher) {
        this.accountRepo = accountRepo;
        this.eventPublisher = eventPublisher;
    }

    @Transactional(rollbackFor = Exception.class)
    public void transfer(String fromId, String toId, BigDecimal amount) {
        Account sender = accountRepo.findByIdForUpdate(fromId);
        Account receiver = accountRepo.findByIdForUpdate(toId);
        sender.debit(amount);
        receiver.credit(amount);

        // Publish event after commit
        TransactionSynchronizationManager.registerSynchronization(
            new TransactionSynchronization() {
                @Override
                public void afterCommit() {
                    eventPublisher.publishEvent(
                        new TransferCompletedEvent(fromId, toId, amount));
                }
            }
        );
    }
}
```
